To solve count-driven clustering and separate CD4+ from CD8+ T cells without an external single-cell reference, use Bayesian topic modeling, count-aware probabilistic models, or targeted marker-guided classification rather than standard HVG-based graph clustering.
Because standard PCA and Leiden/Louvain clustering select genes with the highest global variance, they naturally lock onto total UMI depth (library size) and broad functional states (like cell cycle or exhaustion) rather than subtle, low-count lineage markers.

Reference-Free Tools & Probabilistic Models

• stLearn / BayesSpace / SpatialPCA (Spatially Aware Probabilistic Clustering). Standard clustering treats each segmented cell independently. Spatial clustering models like BayesSpace or SpatialPCA borrow statistical strength from immediate spatial neighbors using Markov random fields or spatial kernels, which helps smooth out severe UMI dropout in low-count T cells without needing a reference.
• CellAssign / Garnett / SCINA (Marker-Driven Probabilistic Classifiers). While technically semi-supervised, these tools are completely reference-free because they only require a simple text list of known positive and negative marker genes (e.g., CD8A, CD8B, GZMK vs. CD4, IL7R, FOXP3). CellAssign uses a hierarchical statistical model that explicitly accounts for library size and negative binomial noise, preventing total UMI count from dominating cell assignments.
• scVI / PeakVI (Deep Generative Modeling). Using scVI (scvi-tools) with n_latent set low and explicitly passing total_counts or library size into the generative model helps disentangle technical sequencing depth from biological signal. Once trained, you can run differential expression or clustering on the latent space where count effects are regressed out.
• Latent Dirichlet Allocation / Topic Modeling (e.g., CountClust,  cisTopic, or MIRA). Topic models decompose each cell's sparse count matrix into overlapping transcriptional "programs" (topics) rather than forcing hard, mutually exclusive clusters. This allows a T cell to simultaneously hold a high score for an "Exhaustion Topic" and a distinct score for a "CD8 Lineage Topic."

Preprocessing Fixes to Eliminate Count-Driven Clustering

If you want to keep using standard graph clustering (Scanpy/Seurat) on your segmented T cells, you must change how features and counts are handled before running PCA:
• Switch to Pearson Residuals (SCTransform or scanpy.experimental.pp.normalize_pearson_residuals). Standard log-normalization (log1p) fails on ultra-sparse Visium HD cells because zero-inflation distorts variance stabilization, causing PCA component 1 to correlate directly with total UMI count. Analytic Pearson residuals explicitly model count depth and prevent high-count cells from forming artificial clusters.
• Replace HVGs with Supervised Feature Selection. Do not run FindVariableFeatures or highly_variable_genes on your T-cell subset. Instead, force PCA and neighbor graph construction to run only on a curated panel of 50–150 T-cell lineage, transcription factor, and effector genes (excluding ribosomal, mitochondrial, heat-shock, and cell-cycle genes).
• Apply Metacell Aggregation (SEACells or SuperCell). Even after cell segmentation, a single T cell in Visium HD may only capture 50–200 UMIs. Grouping transcriptionally and spatially adjacent T cells into small "metacells" (e.g., 5–10 cells per metacell) boosts UMI depth per unit enough for CD4 and CD8A/B co-expression modules to emerge in unsupervised clustering.
